course: dictionary
lesson: 2943
title: A squirrel's home
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A drey is a nest, and the words around it can tell you what it means. Read this: «Squirrels stay in their nest, called a drey, on the coldest days.» The word called is a clue. It tells you the new word is being explained, so a drey is the nest.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. «Squirrels stay in their nest on the coldest days» still makes sense, so nest is the right meaning for drey.
explain: Words also show where and how things happen. The sentence says the squirrels eat «buried nuts and seeds from feeders», so the drey is a home, not a food. Put the clues together and you can work out the meaning.
fact: A drey is the nest of a squirrel, built high in a tree.
fact: A drey is made from twigs, leaves and moss, and it is round like a ball.
fact: A drey has a soft lining inside, where the squirrel sleeps and keeps its babies warm.
fact: Drey is a noun, and its plural is dreys.
fact: A good check for any new word is to swap your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
exercise: «Squirrels stay in their drey on the coldest days.» What is a drey? | A kind of nut | A tall, thin tree trunk | A hole dug under the ground | *A nest built high in a tree
exercise: The drey is round and has a soft lining inside. What is the soft lining for? | To hold the squirrel's food | *To keep the squirrel and its babies warm | To keep rain out of the pond
exercise: Grandad says, «That squirrel has built a drey in the old oak.» Where is the drey? | Inside the house | Under the path | *High in the oak tree | In the river
test: The sentence says «they stay in their nest, called a drey». What does the word called tell you? | It means the drey is under the ground | It shows the drey is for eating | *It shows the new word is being explained: a drey is a nest | It means the drey is a bird
test: Which material is a drey made from? | *Twigs, leaves and moss | Glass | Metal | Plastic
exam: Which is the plural of drey? | *dreys | drey's | drys | dreies
